1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Mechanical beasts that prevent floods, deepen waterways, and build beaches.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:15,000
They cut, scoop, suck, and spew an ocean of mud, silt, and sand.

3
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:21,000
These serpents of slurry are conjuring up new real estate and changing the map of our world.

4
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, Dredging on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Dredging can be defined as the underwater excavation of soil or rock.

6
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:52,680
Pretty simple, though getting the job done is anything but.

7
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:56,860
Dredges can make sand rain from the sky,

8
00:00:56,861 --> 00:01:00,160
clear mud from the biggest of rivers.

9
00:01:00,161 --> 00:01:04,160
They can cross continents and transform underwater mountains

10
00:01:04,161 --> 00:01:07,160
of silk into lush wildlife habitat.

11
00:01:07,161 --> 00:01:11,160
Dredging can compress centuries of geologic evolution

12
00:01:11,161 --> 00:01:14,160
into an almost routine construction project.

13
00:01:14,161 --> 00:01:18,160
Dredges come in two basic types.

14
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:21,160
Mechanical dredges scoop material from the water

15
00:01:21,161 --> 00:01:23,160
with some type of container,

16
00:01:23,161 --> 00:01:28,160
while hydraulic dredges act like giant vacuum cleaners,

17
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:30,960
sucking a mixture of sand, rock, and water

18
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:34,900
from a river, lake, or ocean bottom.

19
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,100
Today's global economy relies on mammoth-sized vessels

20
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:40,720
to move the world's cargo.

21
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:46,620
Big ships need big water, and that means dredging big time.

22
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:49,260
The basic unit of domestic and international shipping

23
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:50,520
is the steel container.

24
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:55,960
These 40-foot-long boxes are filled with everything

25
00:01:55,961 --> 00:01:59,360
from sneakers to Subarus, and then piled on top

26
00:01:59,361 --> 00:02:01,060
of monster-sized ships.

27
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:06,200
What we're finding is, is that many of the vessels

28
00:02:06,201 --> 00:02:09,520
that carry the commodities, oil, food, grain,

29
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:12,840
these types of vessels, the economics for the shippers

30
00:02:12,841 --> 00:02:17,420
tells them to get larger, tells them to get deeper draft.

31
00:02:19,620 --> 00:02:22,600
Draft is the depth of a ship below the water surface.

32
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:29,820
modern harbors are in a constant scramble to keep their waterways deep enough for the drafts of modern container ships.

33
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:34,660
This channel right now is at 45 feet deep.

34
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:40,440
And based on the tremendous import business that the United States does,

35
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:46,320
it's a lot less expensive for the products to come via larger container ships.

36
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:48,880
Larger container ships means deeper draft.

37
00:02:48,881 --> 00:03:02,640
In May 2005, the ports of New York and New Jersey began a joint 10-year, $1.6 billion project to deepen the main shipping channels into the harbor to 50 feet.

38
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:12,240
This particular deepening project is one of the top two civil works projects in the nation.

39
00:03:12,241 --> 00:03:17,920
One-third of the nation is touched by the commerce that comes through this port.

40
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:21,820
In the Kill Van Cull section of the harbor,

41
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:28,440
an array of dynamite charges is used to loosen the hard rock below the surface.

42
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:36,140
That fractures all of the rock into maybe football-size, basketball-size chunks,

43
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:39,620
and then a backhoe goes in and lifts that material out.

44
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:45,500
But the largest backhoe dredge in the world, named the New York,

45
00:03:45,860 --> 00:03:47,960
don't need no stinking dynamite.

46
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:53,880
We're able to dig rock that other machines would need to have blasted in order for them to dig it.

47
00:03:54,220 --> 00:03:58,980
You see the bucket behind me, it's got the ability to cut into a sheer face of rock,

48
00:03:59,340 --> 00:04:02,640
and depending on the strength of that rock, actually break it as it goes along,

49
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:05,520
thereby getting rid of the need to have to get blasted.

50
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:11,740
This bucket can pick up up to 26 yards, and once we got the whole load,

51
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:17,820
the boom, the stick, the bucket, and the material, we're picking up close to 260 tons.

52
00:04:17,821 --> 00:04:19,820
Every 50 seconds.

53
00:04:19,821 --> 00:04:22,820
The excavator itself has got two diesel engines

54
00:04:22,821 --> 00:04:24,820
with a total of 3,000 horsepower.

55
00:04:24,821 --> 00:04:28,820
The breakout force is approximately 370,000 pounds.

56
00:04:28,821 --> 00:04:32,820
That means 370,000 pounds are applied to the end of the shovel

57
00:04:32,821 --> 00:04:35,820
in order to break up the rock.

58
00:04:35,821 --> 00:04:38,820
Like right now, I'm booming down, and I'm sticking in.

59
00:04:38,821 --> 00:04:41,820
And at the same time, you curl the bucket,

60
00:04:41,821 --> 00:04:43,820
and you're watching your computer,

61
00:04:43,821 --> 00:04:45,820
and you're picking up the material now.

62
00:04:45,821 --> 00:04:47,820
So then you just boom up.

63
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:53,820
The bucket's pretty heavy, so you just push this button,

64
00:04:53,821 --> 00:04:56,820
and then it gives you more power to pick up.

65
00:04:56,821 --> 00:04:59,820
This is where the action takes place.

66
00:04:59,821 --> 00:05:02,820
We have on the very end are the bucket teeth,

67
00:05:02,821 --> 00:05:04,820
which weigh about 80 to 100 pounds a piece.

68
00:05:04,821 --> 00:05:08,820
This edge of this tooth breaks into the gaps in the rock

69
00:05:08,821 --> 00:05:11,820
and helps shovel the rock into the back of the bucket.

70
00:05:11,821 --> 00:05:14,820
Those are connected to the lip by these adapters.

71
00:05:14,821 --> 00:05:19,820
which are also massive steel weighing about 1,000 pounds apiece.

72
00:05:19,821 --> 00:05:23,820
This is where the 370,000 pounds of breakout force hits the rock.

73
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:31,820
The New York and other dredges are constantly working to counteract the forces in action under the water's surface.

74
00:05:31,821 --> 00:05:37,820
Without dredging, silt would fill in these waterways and make navigation impossible.

75
00:05:37,821 --> 00:05:44,580
Across the continent from where the New York is working, another dredge defends the port

76
00:05:44,581 --> 00:05:47,480
of Oakland, California.

77
00:05:47,481 --> 00:05:51,420
Even though it's called the Florida, this vessel operates primarily up and down the

78
00:05:51,421 --> 00:05:54,260
west coast of the United States.

79
00:05:54,261 --> 00:05:58,760
The Florida is what's known as a cutter suction dredge.

80
00:05:58,761 --> 00:06:02,980
Thanks to its efficiency and versatility, this is the most commonly used type of dredge

81
00:06:02,981 --> 00:06:07,280
in the United States.

82
00:06:07,281 --> 00:06:12,280
The cutter head suction dredge has a revolving head at the business end that dislodges sand

83
00:06:12,281 --> 00:06:15,600
and rock from the bottom of the waterway.

84
00:06:15,601 --> 00:06:22,000
The cutter head revolves around a mouth that is attached to a powerful suction pump.

85
00:06:22,001 --> 00:06:26,500
As material is dislodged by the cutting action, it's sucked up through a pipe connected to

86
00:06:26,501 --> 00:06:33,400
a barge or disposal area on the water's surface.

87
00:06:33,401 --> 00:06:37,480
As it drenches the main shipping channel in the port of Oakland, the Florida depends on

88
00:06:37,481 --> 00:06:40,560
every member of its 16-person crew.

89
00:06:40,561 --> 00:06:46,160
But the operator known as the lever man has an especially complex task.

90
00:06:46,161 --> 00:06:51,760
He's responsible for our presence in the ship channel, so he's monitoring the radio

91
00:06:51,761 --> 00:06:55,420
for navigation interests and he's got to be prepared for ships to pass.

92
00:06:55,421 --> 00:06:59,660
From a production standpoint, he's looking at whatever the limit might be, and in some

93
00:06:59,661 --> 00:07:03,780
In some cases it's the material is so tough that his limit is he's using all the cutter

94
00:07:03,781 --> 00:07:04,780
power he's got.

95
00:07:04,781 --> 00:07:08,240
Or in some cases it might be that he's just swinging as fast as he can and we're limited

96
00:07:08,241 --> 00:07:09,900
by how much ground we can cover.

97
00:07:09,901 --> 00:07:15,160
You've got to have a mental image picture of what your cutter is doing and how hard

98
00:07:15,161 --> 00:07:20,400
it actually is cutting in the material so you're actually mentally where the cutter

99
00:07:20,401 --> 00:07:21,400
is.

100
00:07:21,401 --> 00:07:24,340
You can feel the force that is going into the bank.

101
00:07:24,341 --> 00:07:27,840
You can tell by the cutter amps how much, it gives you an indication.

102
00:07:27,841 --> 00:07:31,900
I can feel on this joystick how much tension there is where the cutter is.

103
00:07:33,100 --> 00:07:35,780
It's got 17,000 installed horsepower.

104
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:40,860
It's got a 3,000 horsepower cutter head, 2,000 horsepower ladder pump,

105
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:43,200
and a 10,000 horsepower main pump.

106
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:45,880
It pumps through a 34-inch pipeline,

107
00:07:46,340 --> 00:07:49,360
so a lot of times you'll hear it called a 34-inch hydraulic dredge

108
00:07:49,361 --> 00:07:51,500
or a 34-inch cutter suction dredge.

109
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:54,380
It's one of the largest cutter suction dredges in the nation.

110
00:07:54,381 --> 00:08:01,380
The Florida's main attraction is a nasty looking beast with interchangeable heads.

111
00:08:01,381 --> 00:08:05,380
It's about 15 tons of steel.

112
00:08:05,381 --> 00:08:12,380
It's a five arm cutter head and it's got what we have right now on it are wide flared teeth

113
00:08:12,381 --> 00:08:15,380
and these are what we call the spades.

114
00:08:15,381 --> 00:08:19,380
As that cutter head is turning, what actually digs the material is this tooth system.

115
00:08:19,381 --> 00:08:26,380
When they're on the clock, these iron monsters work 24-7 to achieve maximum productivity.

116
00:08:26,381 --> 00:08:32,380
The only thing that'll stop the sucking is a breakdown, like this one.

117
00:08:32,381 --> 00:08:36,380
The Florida's cutterhead tried to bite off more rock than it could chew.

118
00:08:36,381 --> 00:08:44,285
It encounters something substantial enough, as this cutterhead's rotating, you can actually break the tooth system, which is what happened here, and that's the reason

119
00:08:44,286 --> 00:08:45,380
we took this off today.

120
00:08:45,381 --> 00:08:51,900
this off today. But the work never stops, thanks to the dredge's unique power plant.

121
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:57,460
The Florida is one of the only electric dredges in the nation, and draws its strength from a

122
00:08:57,461 --> 00:08:59,862
dedicated substation back on shore.

123
00:08:59,863 --> 00:09:03,980
We bring 14,000 volts out to the dredge via submarine cable.

124
00:09:04,540 --> 00:09:07,288
We have about 15,000 feet of that cable.

125
00:09:07,289 --> 00:09:10,860
This makes the ship of great value in urban harbors,

126
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,360
where the pollution of massive diesel engines running around the clock would be a deal breaker.

127
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:24,100
A few miles from where the Florida is working, another dredge plies the cold outer waters of the San Francisco Bay.

128
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:29,440
The Essayons is what's known as a hopper suction dredge,

129
00:09:30,100 --> 00:09:36,740
a self-powered vessel capable of storing dredged material on board in a huge holding tank, or hopper,

130
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:39,420
and then transporting it to a disposal area.

131
00:09:39,421 --> 00:09:44,880
Hopper dredges are the weapon of choice when dredged material needs to be transported more

132
00:09:44,881 --> 00:09:52,580
than a few miles and are often used to clear offshore sandbars.

133
00:09:52,581 --> 00:09:57,780
The hopper dredge works by making repeated passes over the target area.

134
00:09:57,781 --> 00:10:03,300
Two suction pumps called drag arms are hinged on each side of the ship.

135
00:10:03,301 --> 00:10:07,780
The intake openings are dragged along the bottom as the vessel moves forward at speeds

136
00:10:07,781 --> 00:10:11,460
up to three miles per hour.

137
00:10:11,461 --> 00:10:13,620
The dredged material is sucked up the pipe

138
00:10:13,621 --> 00:10:16,260
and deposited in the hopper.

139
00:10:16,261 --> 00:10:18,920
Once full, the dredge moves to the disposal area

140
00:10:18,921 --> 00:10:21,500
to unload before starting the whole process again.

141
00:10:24,260 --> 00:10:27,140
The Hopper Dredge Essayance is the most technologically

142
00:10:27,141 --> 00:10:30,780
advanced dredge in the American fleet.

143
00:10:30,781 --> 00:10:35,580
We've got all of the latest modern electronics on board here.

144
00:10:35,581 --> 00:10:37,500
We have automatic radar plotting aids.

145
00:10:37,501 --> 00:10:41,380
We have automatic vessel identification systems.

146
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:48,400
We have engine room monitoring systems to control all of the engines, the RPMs, the speeds, the pitch.

147
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:50,220
It's all controlled electronically from up here.

148
00:10:50,760 --> 00:10:53,600
We have electronic control of all of the functions.

149
00:10:53,780 --> 00:10:57,600
We'll know about watertight doors, fire safety, pumps, valves.

150
00:10:59,020 --> 00:11:02,820
The sophisticated electronics do more than control the movement of the ship.

151
00:11:02,821 --> 00:11:09,440
The newest technology makes visible what dredge operators could only imagine for hundreds of years.

152
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:11,661
This is called the pit.

153
00:11:11,662 --> 00:11:15,560
This is where the dredge control officer takes care of all the dredging operations.

154
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:19,360
He's controlling two drag arms, one on the port, one on the starboard,

155
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:23,940
and he monitors the whole control situation from up here.

156
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,760
He's got three screens in front of him, and he's monitoring the dredging process.

157
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:33,120
This is the DTPS system, and it shows the ship going back and forth in the channel,

158
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:36,280
and the different colors represent the different soundings from the survey.

159
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:39,840
And as you can see, as we're going back and forth, it's turned these areas to blue,

160
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:43,280
which are the deeper areas, and as they become blue, we stay away from them,

161
00:11:43,281 --> 00:11:47,080
and we just concentrate on bringing the ship through the yellow and the green until we turn them blue,

162
00:11:47,700 --> 00:11:49,120
and then when it's all done, we move on.

163
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:54,940
Smaller and faster computers have led to sophisticated instrumentation

164
00:11:54,941 --> 00:11:58,680
that allows dredging work to take place in a way never before possible.

165
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:04,760
These modern instruments give the Essayons a big advantage in the open ocean.

166
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:10,640
The north coast here, we get predominantly high northwest winds during the summer,

167
00:12:11,180 --> 00:12:14,860
and they'll blow 25 knots most afternoons, so you get a good swell coming in.

168
00:12:15,520 --> 00:12:20,460
We'll operate in swells up to 12, maybe push it up to 15 feet before we'll run off of a bar.

169
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:22,260
We have swell compensators.

170
00:12:22,261 --> 00:12:25,400
They're these large hydraulic rams that run up and down on the deck.

171
00:12:25,401 --> 00:12:27,900
And what they do is when the ship is operating in a swell,

172
00:12:27,901 --> 00:12:30,560
she'll heave up and down on the dredge site,

173
00:12:30,561 --> 00:12:32,400
and the drag head is on the bottom.

174
00:12:32,401 --> 00:12:35,800
What the swell compensator does is that it runs up and down automatically

175
00:12:35,801 --> 00:12:39,100
and keeps the wires taut on the winches so that they don't spool out,

176
00:12:39,101 --> 00:12:42,820
and you get a bird nest on the winch, which can make a day pretty miserable.

177
00:12:42,821 --> 00:12:46,620
Modern dredging has its share of challenges.

178
00:12:46,621 --> 00:12:51,360
But over 100 years ago, the world's biggest ever construction project

179
00:12:51,361 --> 00:12:54,700
almost proved too much for an army of dredges.

180
00:12:56,800 --> 00:12:59,100
To maintain navigable waterways,

181
00:12:59,240 --> 00:13:02,100
approximately 300 million cubic yards of material,

182
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:04,980
enough to fill about 3 million backyard swimming pools,

183
00:13:05,340 --> 00:13:07,540
are dredged in the United States every year.

184
00:13:09,940 --> 00:13:12,020
Dredges may look prehistoric,

185
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:14,500
but the guts of the modern dredge

186
00:13:14,501 --> 00:13:17,120
reflect some very sophisticated research and development.

187
00:13:17,121 --> 00:13:24,440
The Center for Dredging Studies at Texas A&M University is a landlocked facility that

188
00:13:24,441 --> 00:13:32,160
houses a remarkable array of equipment and monitoring devices.

189
00:13:32,161 --> 00:13:39,780
The coastal engineering lab at the center boasts a 75 by 120 foot wave basin.

190
00:13:39,781 --> 00:13:44,520
The lab also includes an experimental dredging tank, complete with a custom-built cutter

191
00:13:44,521 --> 00:13:47,420
suction dredge unit.

192
00:13:47,421 --> 00:13:50,620
It's a scale model of a real dredge with all the same function.

193
00:13:50,621 --> 00:13:54,520
So here in clear water and, let's say, clean sand,

194
00:13:54,521 --> 00:13:56,400
you can actually see a lot more than in the reality

195
00:13:56,401 --> 00:14:00,740
and do research how certain things behave in the reality.

196
00:14:00,741 --> 00:14:02,940
As the simulator's operating, it's

197
00:14:02,941 --> 00:14:06,500
going to be run back and forth sweeping motion in a pit

198
00:14:06,501 --> 00:14:08,240
through different types of material,

199
00:14:08,241 --> 00:14:10,540
whether it be a clay or sand or gravel,

200
00:14:10,541 --> 00:14:12,280
depending upon what type of simulation

201
00:14:12,281 --> 00:14:14,680
that they're running their tests under.

202
00:14:14,681 --> 00:14:17,680
It's of interest to know what size forces you need

203
00:14:17,681 --> 00:14:19,380
and how much power you need to, let's say,

204
00:14:19,381 --> 00:14:22,780
cut a certain amount of cubic yards per hour,

205
00:14:22,781 --> 00:14:25,920
certain production, and if you can get a handle

206
00:14:25,921 --> 00:14:28,100
on how certain things behave, you can scale that up

207
00:14:28,101 --> 00:14:31,340
into a real design of larger dredging equipment.

208
00:14:31,341 --> 00:14:32,740
Cut the suction dredges in its functions

209
00:14:32,741 --> 00:14:34,340
have been the same for years.

210
00:14:37,700 --> 00:14:39,980
Data logging, it has automated movements

211
00:14:39,981 --> 00:14:41,940
and state-of-the-art production monitoring

212
00:14:41,941 --> 00:14:43,034
and controlled.

213
00:14:43,035 --> 00:14:48,940
Today's cutting-edge technology is the result of thousands of years of innovation

214
00:14:48,941 --> 00:14:50,207
and refinement.

215
00:14:50,208 --> 00:14:56,540
As early as 5000 BC, the ancient Egyptians tried to control the flow of the

216
00:14:56,541 --> 00:15:03,817
Nile River by building canals to divert annual floodwaters to distant fields.

217
00:15:03,818 --> 00:15:05,140
And each year,

218
00:15:05,141 --> 00:15:07,629
these canals had to be cleared of silt.

219
00:15:07,630 --> 00:15:11,140
This was one of our first organized dredging practices.

220
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:18,940
Egyptian farmers used primitive hand dredges, basically cloth bags at the end of poles that

221
00:15:19,740 --> 00:15:22,521
were dragged along the canal bottom.

222
00:15:22,522 --> 00:15:26,460
But it was the eastern Mediterranean that gave rise

223
00:15:26,461 --> 00:15:29,793
to the first marine dredging technology.

224
00:15:29,794 --> 00:15:33,460
Solid archaeological evidence indicates that

225
00:15:35,540 --> 00:15:41,820
the Phoenicians built artificial harbors at Sidon and Tyre in the 13th century BC.

226
00:15:41,821 --> 00:15:43,208
what is now Lebanon.

227
00:15:43,209 --> 00:15:48,480
Mainly it was for commerce, you know, bringing in big clay jars full of wine

228
00:15:48,481 --> 00:15:50,678
and olive oil, bringing in troop movement.

229
00:15:50,679 --> 00:15:53,400
A lot of times it's war has to do with the dredging.

230
00:15:53,820 --> 00:15:57,580
You know, the bigger ships carrying all of their horses and their armor and their soldiers,

231
00:15:58,040 --> 00:16:02,180
the bigger their galleys got, the deeper they had to go in the harbors that they were running,

232
00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:08,460
running up a lot of these shallow rivers. The Phoenicians were probably the first to

233
00:16:08,461 --> 00:16:10,460
from a platform out in the water.

234
00:16:10,461 --> 00:16:12,460
Basically, it was a flat-bottom barge.

235
00:16:12,461 --> 00:16:15,460
They would have a big tripod set up in the center of this scow,

236
00:16:15,461 --> 00:16:17,460
and they would have a long rod on it.

237
00:16:17,461 --> 00:16:21,460
On one end of it, you would have a hide bag or a cloth weave bag

238
00:16:21,461 --> 00:16:23,460
that they would dip down into the bottom.

239
00:16:23,461 --> 00:16:25,460
A couple of big guys would move the arm,

240
00:16:25,461 --> 00:16:27,460
it would drag across the bottom, fill up,

241
00:16:27,461 --> 00:16:31,460
they'd swing it up, bring it on deck, drop it, scoop it out,

242
00:16:31,461 --> 00:16:33,460
and take it back out.

243
00:16:33,461 --> 00:16:37,460
The Roman Empire absorbed this new technology

244
00:16:37,461 --> 00:16:41,080
and applied its own refinements based on the availability of slave labor.

245
00:16:43,220 --> 00:16:45,940
When they had their empire and they were plundering everybody,

246
00:16:46,040 --> 00:16:47,280
they had to get everything out of the countries.

247
00:16:47,580 --> 00:16:51,000
And so they had ports that weren't deep enough for their galleys to come in.

248
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:53,920
In fact, the Romans were very good construction engineers.

249
00:16:54,320 --> 00:16:56,280
They used concrete over 2,000 years ago.

250
00:16:56,340 --> 00:16:57,700
They would build quays and piers.

251
00:16:59,760 --> 00:17:03,220
By 400 A.D., the Romans had built lighthouses

252
00:17:03,221 --> 00:17:05,800
from the Mediterranean all the way to the North Sea.

253
00:17:05,801 --> 00:17:13,760
But without systematic dredging, the harbors they protected were threatened and ultimately destroyed by siltation.

254
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:24,140
Throughout the Middle Ages, European innovators made crude attempts to try to combat the relentless forces of wind and water.

255
00:17:25,620 --> 00:17:31,440
These included the dredging rake, operated from a riverbank by eight men trudging in a circle.

256
00:17:31,441 --> 00:17:38,720
Because the Netherlands, or low countries, are below sea level, they had even more reason

257
00:17:38,721 --> 00:17:42,440
than most to advance the art of dredging.

258
00:17:42,441 --> 00:17:47,860
In the 16th century, Dutch engineers developed the mud mill, essentially an endless chain

259
00:17:47,861 --> 00:17:51,780
of digging planks driven by men and horses.

260
00:17:51,781 --> 00:17:58,180
They looked like a wheel that you would use with chipmunks,

261
00:17:58,700 --> 00:18:05,340
and as the wheel would go around, this had paddles attached to it.

262
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:08,620
It was very labor-intensive and very similar to what we do now.

263
00:18:08,621 --> 00:18:09,431
Very repetitive.

264
00:18:09,432 --> 00:18:13,180
Around and around you would go, all day, all night, until you got it down.

265
00:18:14,600 --> 00:18:19,920
In the 17th century, a Frenchman named Denis Papin invented the centrifugal pump,

266
00:18:19,921 --> 00:18:24,320
which used a spinning motion to move a liquid from the source to the delivery point.

267
00:18:25,880 --> 00:18:29,960
The design allowed for the transport of large quantities of water mixed with sediment.

268
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:36,400
But it wasn't until the development of steam-driven engines in the mid-19th century

269
00:18:36,401 --> 00:18:39,140
that dredging became a force to be reckoned with.

270
00:18:39,820 --> 00:18:43,720
The steam engine was a real big technological breakthrough at the time

271
00:18:43,721 --> 00:18:48,660
because it got rid of the five horses and the feed and the sanitary problems associated with that

272
00:18:48,661 --> 00:18:50,780
and allowed them to get bigger, bigger pumps.

273
00:18:51,040 --> 00:18:52,280
Bigger pumps mean more material.

274
00:18:52,840 --> 00:18:58,120
The steam power gave them the ability to have bigger equipment,

275
00:18:58,121 --> 00:19:03,700
bigger buckets, and have the suction on suction dredges

276
00:19:03,701 --> 00:19:07,880
so that they could excavate more material

277
00:19:07,881 --> 00:19:11,020
with less energy and equipment.

278
00:19:12,620 --> 00:19:14,700
In the mid-19th century,

279
00:19:15,220 --> 00:19:17,960
the digging of the Suez Canal marked a turning point

280
00:19:17,961 --> 00:19:19,100
in the history of dredging.

281
00:19:20,180 --> 00:19:22,280
Work began in 1854,

282
00:19:23,100 --> 00:19:25,180
using mostly animal and slave labor.

283
00:19:26,120 --> 00:19:28,140
But it wasn't until 15 years later

284
00:19:28,141 --> 00:19:28,960
that it was completed,

285
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:32,080
thanks in large part to steam-powered bucket dredges.

286
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:38,120
The completion of this 91-mile canal in 1869

287
00:19:38,121 --> 00:19:41,360
shortened the sea route between Europe and the Far East

288
00:19:41,361 --> 00:19:43,000
by 5,000 miles.

289
00:19:45,120 --> 00:19:46,960
In the early 20th century,

290
00:19:46,961 --> 00:19:48,720
it was America's turn to shine

291
00:19:48,721 --> 00:19:51,680
in a small tropical country called Panama.

292
00:19:53,900 --> 00:19:56,320
A route across the Central American Isthmus

293
00:19:56,321 --> 00:19:58,440
was first considered in the 16th century.

294
00:20:00,700 --> 00:20:03,040
Spanish rulers realized they could transport

295
00:20:03,041 --> 00:20:05,620
the riches of Asia back to Spain much more quickly

296
00:20:05,621 --> 00:20:08,420
if they could circumvent the South American continent.

297
00:20:10,680 --> 00:20:12,420
Finally, in the 19th century,

298
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:15,560
the French developed what seemed like a workable plan

299
00:20:15,561 --> 00:20:16,280
for a crossing.

300
00:20:16,960 --> 00:20:26,180
After their success with the Suez Canal, the French were confident they could repeat the achievement and build a waterway through 51 miles of Panamanian jungle.

301
00:20:27,660 --> 00:20:34,560
But tropical disease and accidents took the lives of 25,000 people in less than 10 years.

302
00:20:35,880 --> 00:20:37,147
In 1904, the U.S.

303
00:20:37,148 --> 00:20:43,480
government bought the French company's assets for $40 million and began construction.

304
00:20:44,440 --> 00:20:48,480
Included in the deal were seven bucket ladder dredges used in the first attempt.

305
00:20:49,920 --> 00:20:53,520
The bucket ladder dredge was the earliest form of mechanical dredge.

306
00:20:55,240 --> 00:21:00,560
It uses a continuous chain of buckets rotating around a rigid frame called a ladder.

307
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:07,460
When the ladder is lowered to the bottom at an angle, the empty buckets ride down and dig into the mud.

308
00:21:07,461 --> 00:21:14,460
The loaded buckets then return along the upper side of the ladder and dump out at the top.

309
00:21:14,461 --> 00:21:21,460
As work on the canal dragged on, project engineers searched for a more effective machine to combat

310
00:21:21,461 --> 00:21:24,460
the thick Panamanian muck.

311
00:21:24,461 --> 00:21:30,460
The Ellicott Machine Corporation of Baltimore, Maryland was enlisted to custom build six

312
00:21:30,461 --> 00:21:32,460
cutter suction dredges.

313
00:21:32,461 --> 00:21:36,460
They got a lot of high horsepower and the pumps could get bigger and bigger and bigger,

314
00:21:36,461 --> 00:21:40,860
and bigger, and they started moving more and more material.

315
00:21:40,861 --> 00:21:43,660
With a major assist from the Alicot dredges,

316
00:21:43,661 --> 00:21:47,500
the canal was finally completed in 1914.

317
00:21:47,501 --> 00:21:50,700
It was the largest engineering project of its time

318
00:21:50,701 --> 00:21:56,420
and involved the clearing of 100 million cubic yards of dirt.

319
00:21:56,421 --> 00:21:58,680
The cutter suction dredge that earned its stripes

320
00:21:58,681 --> 00:22:02,120
in the Panama Canal hasn't changed its basic design

321
00:22:02,121 --> 00:22:05,620
all that much in over a century.

322
00:22:05,621 --> 00:22:10,840
But the scale of today's cutters makes projects that were once impossible commonplace.

323
00:22:12,820 --> 00:22:19,140
Although the Panama Canal can accommodate ships carrying up to 65,000 tons of cargo, stores, and fuel,

324
00:22:19,700 --> 00:22:23,160
modern ocean vessels can carry more than five times that amount.

325
00:22:28,340 --> 00:22:31,940
As sure as beads will fall on Bourbon Street during Mardi Gras,

326
00:22:31,941 --> 00:22:36,940
The Mississippi River will need dredging year-round.

327
00:22:36,941 --> 00:22:38,940
Army Corps dredges work constantly

328
00:22:38,941 --> 00:22:40,940
to keep the Mississippi River moving.

329
00:22:40,941 --> 00:22:44,940
About 200 million tons of cargo came by this point

330
00:22:44,941 --> 00:22:46,940
by barge last year.

331
00:22:46,941 --> 00:22:49,940
In order to understand how much cargo that is,

332
00:22:49,941 --> 00:22:52,940
it would take a truck every three seconds

333
00:22:52,941 --> 00:22:55,940
rolling down the highway to move that material.

334
00:22:55,941 --> 00:22:58,940
That's a truck every three seconds, 24 hours a day,

335
00:22:58,941 --> 00:23:02,940
24 hours a day, seven days a week, 365 days a year.

336
00:23:02,941 --> 00:23:07,940
Well, right now we are about a mile away from where La Salle

337
00:23:07,941 --> 00:23:11,940
planted the pole in the name of France in 1683.

338
00:23:11,941 --> 00:23:15,940
And since that time, this river has created huge channels

339
00:23:15,941 --> 00:23:18,940
of navigation for the United States.

340
00:23:18,941 --> 00:23:22,940
The Mississippi River drains 41% of the territory of the United States.

341
00:23:22,941 --> 00:23:24,940
That's a lot of area.

342
00:23:24,941 --> 00:23:27,940
Once it rains or ice melts in those areas,

343
00:23:27,941 --> 00:23:29,640
is going to go through the Mississippi River.

344
00:23:29,641 --> 00:23:33,880
It carries a lot of water, but also a lot of sediment.

345
00:23:33,881 --> 00:23:36,680
The Eagle One is a hopper dredge.

346
00:23:36,681 --> 00:23:39,980
Like other hopper dredges, it's self-powered

347
00:23:39,981 --> 00:23:44,860
and deposits its dredged material in a disposal area.

348
00:23:44,861 --> 00:23:46,960
The hopper dredge is an excellent choice

349
00:23:46,961 --> 00:23:50,320
for this type of work because it's in a very busy channel,

350
00:23:50,321 --> 00:23:52,460
and a stationary dredge would have difficulty

351
00:23:52,461 --> 00:23:54,200
with the traffic in the river.

352
00:23:54,201 --> 00:23:56,000
A hopper dredge is maneuverable.

353
00:23:56,001 --> 00:23:57,560
bullets can avoid the other traffic.

354
00:23:58,300 --> 00:24:04,420
Right now we're dredging, and this is the slurry that's coming in from the two main

355
00:24:04,421 --> 00:24:06,280
pumps that we pump it up.

356
00:24:06,281 --> 00:24:11,440
What we do is we suck up the material from the drag arms that are over, that went over

357
00:24:11,441 --> 00:24:12,141
on the side.

358
00:24:12,700 --> 00:24:15,480
It goes through the pumps and it comes down these chutes into the hopper.

359
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:22,080
On a good day, the Eagle One can clear 5,000 dump truck loads worth of material from the

360
00:24:22,081 --> 00:24:22,781
river.

361
00:24:22,782 --> 00:24:31,260
If the drag heads get into thick sediment and sufficient power isn't applied to keep the ship moving forward,

362
00:24:31,760 --> 00:24:36,820
the powerful river current will snap the drag arm guide wires, a very costly accident.

363
00:24:38,520 --> 00:24:42,700
It's this type of risk that demands a highly skilled operator to man the controls.

364
00:24:43,700 --> 00:24:49,180
The wenches work at a certain speed, so I've been doing this since about 1984.

365
00:24:49,181 --> 00:24:52,400
So I know that meant speed right there is about two feet.

366
00:24:53,040 --> 00:24:54,100
That's six inches.

367
00:24:54,940 --> 00:24:58,960
So without looking, I got a pretty good idea how much I just came up.

368
00:24:59,900 --> 00:25:03,840
The Eagle One toils round the clock at work that's never finished,

369
00:25:04,440 --> 00:25:06,940
but that started over 100 years ago.

370
00:25:08,220 --> 00:25:13,920
Dredging in the river was not really accomplished until about 1882.

371
00:25:13,921 --> 00:25:20,040
Up until that time, the channels were blocked by the natural sandbars,

372
00:25:20,220 --> 00:25:23,700
and the draft across those bars was about 12 feet of water.

373
00:25:24,680 --> 00:25:28,300
But recurrent flooding decimated surrounding land areas.

374
00:25:28,980 --> 00:25:33,820
The Great Flood of 1927 covered 23,000 square miles

375
00:25:33,821 --> 00:25:37,180
and forced 700,000 people from their homes.

376
00:25:38,400 --> 00:25:40,600
Soon after this catastrophic event,

377
00:25:40,601 --> 00:25:44,080
Congress passed the 1928 Flood Control Act,

378
00:25:44,081 --> 00:25:47,040
which gave responsibility for maintaining the river

379
00:25:47,041 --> 00:25:49,440
to the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers.

380
00:25:51,740 --> 00:25:53,480
In the decades that followed,

381
00:25:54,040 --> 00:25:56,920
the Corps spent billions of dollars on year-round dredging

382
00:25:56,921 --> 00:25:59,640
to control flooding and assist ship traffic

383
00:25:59,641 --> 00:26:00,960
through this critical waterway.

384
00:26:02,500 --> 00:26:04,200
It's an ongoing process.

385
00:26:04,440 --> 00:26:07,440
Every year, the rains and snow melt up north,

386
00:26:07,520 --> 00:26:10,220
and it washes all the siltation down to the river here,

387
00:26:10,221 --> 00:26:13,220
and dredges like ourselves have to come in

388
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:17,020
and suck up that material that's shoaled back in

389
00:26:17,120 --> 00:26:21,360
to get it back to 51 feet so the ships can come in.

390
00:26:21,460 --> 00:26:23,560
When the Eagle One has filled up the hopper,

391
00:26:23,660 --> 00:26:27,780
it heads out to an open water disposal site.

392
00:26:27,880 --> 00:26:33,080
The Eagle One has a nifty design feature known as a split hull.

393
00:26:33,180 --> 00:26:36,420
The ship is able to mechanically separate into two sections,

394
00:26:36,520 --> 00:26:40,220
allowing all that silt to sink safely to the ocean bottom.

395
00:26:40,221 --> 00:26:47,220
A few miles up river, the dredge Jadwin gets down and dirty.

396
00:26:47,221 --> 00:26:53,220
Less shipping traffic and a more silty bottom in this section of the river call for a different

397
00:26:53,221 --> 00:26:54,493
type of machine.

398
00:26:54,494 --> 00:27:00,220
The Jadwin is a dustpan dredge, named for the shape of its suction head.

399
00:27:00,221 --> 00:27:06,220
Dustpan dredges were designed for the Mississippi River and first built by the Army Corps of

400
00:27:06,221 --> 00:27:10,260
Army Corps of Engineers to fill the need for a dredge that could operate in shallow water

401
00:27:10,261 --> 00:27:13,900
and still be large enough to quickly excavate the shipping channels.

402
00:27:17,900 --> 00:27:23,100
The Jadwin is positioned in the channel using two large anchors that are sunk into the bottom,

403
00:27:23,580 --> 00:27:25,800
about a half mile above where the dredge will be working.

404
00:27:28,100 --> 00:27:32,480
Cables running from the anchors back to the dredge are used to drag the dredge forward

405
00:27:32,481 --> 00:27:35,440
while its powerful pumps are working to suck up the debris.

406
00:27:36,220 --> 00:27:43,220
This is where it all happens.

407
00:27:43,221 --> 00:27:47,940
This is the business end of the dredge, the dustpan that actually scoops up the material

408
00:27:47,941 --> 00:27:50,140
off the bottom of the river.

409
00:27:50,141 --> 00:27:54,460
We inject the water into the sand with this high-pressure water jet.

410
00:27:54,461 --> 00:27:59,120
Have you ever taken a water hose and squirted it straight down into the soil?

411
00:27:59,121 --> 00:28:04,600
Well, that's essentially what these water jets are doing down on the river bottom.

412
00:28:04,601 --> 00:28:11,920
They are fluidizing that sand so that it can be sucked up by the dredge pump.

413
00:28:11,921 --> 00:28:15,960
The slurry dumps into a holding tank before being pumped out.

414
00:28:15,961 --> 00:28:19,880
When maintenance work happens here, everyone on board holds his breath.

415
00:28:19,881 --> 00:28:26,520
This part of the dredge up here is the most dangerous part of the dredge, we think.

416
00:28:26,521 --> 00:28:32,180
If you fall in right here, you don't have a very good chance of getting out without

417
00:28:32,181 --> 00:28:35,420
going all the way through the dredge and through the propellers in the back.

418
00:28:35,960 --> 00:28:37,720
So we put these safety ropes here.

419
00:28:38,400 --> 00:28:40,060
We call them the Jesus ropes.

420
00:28:40,800 --> 00:28:43,040
If you fall in the water and you miss that rope,

421
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:45,140
Jesus is the only thing that can save you.

422
00:28:45,900 --> 00:28:50,180
A pipeline carries the slurry several hundred yards away from the shipping channel.

423
00:28:55,240 --> 00:29:00,900
We're at the end of the pipeline where the material comes out of the pipe and goes into the river.

424
00:29:01,440 --> 00:29:04,520
When the material exits the pipe, it strikes a baffle plate,

425
00:29:05,120 --> 00:29:07,300
and we can change the angle of this baffle plate

426
00:29:07,301 --> 00:29:10,600
and actually steer the pipeline up the river

427
00:29:10,601 --> 00:29:13,720
so that we can discharge this material where we want it to go.

428
00:29:15,220 --> 00:29:20,040
In the past, Mississippi muck was dumped wherever it was easiest and cheapest.

429
00:29:20,960 --> 00:29:25,260
But in the 1990s, state and federal agencies realized

430
00:29:25,261 --> 00:29:28,740
that they were wasting a valuable natural resource.

431
00:29:30,900 --> 00:29:37,680
The Army Corps of Engineers oversees all dredging in the 25,000 miles of commercially

432
00:29:37,681 --> 00:29:43,060
navigable U.S. waterways.

433
00:29:43,061 --> 00:29:47,780
Since underwater silt and sediment are often the final resting place for toxic waste, it

434
00:29:47,781 --> 00:29:50,820
is washed down from industrial sites.

435
00:29:50,821 --> 00:29:56,740
Modern dredging can have dire consequences if the right precautions are not taken.

436
00:29:56,741 --> 00:29:59,740
The massive deepening project in the New York, New Jersey harbor

437
00:29:59,741 --> 00:30:03,140
impacts several areas that contain toxic materials.

438
00:30:04,580 --> 00:30:09,620
For decades, industrial waste trickled down the Hudson River and its tributaries,

439
00:30:10,200 --> 00:30:15,060
creating a dangerous layer of contaminated sediment in some parts of the New York, New Jersey port.

440
00:30:16,580 --> 00:30:24,620
This New York harbor, New York area, of course, was very heavily industrialized in the turn of the century.

441
00:30:24,621 --> 00:30:29,280
and we're dealing with that industrial legacy right now.

442
00:30:29,720 --> 00:30:33,200
Being the top layer of silt sediment material in the bay

443
00:30:33,201 --> 00:30:37,580
and being that this is a very industrialized and urbanized bay,

444
00:30:38,260 --> 00:30:40,560
this top layer does have contamination in it.

445
00:30:41,920 --> 00:30:44,560
While the backhoe drench does a superb job

446
00:30:44,561 --> 00:30:47,780
grinding out the hard rock sections of the New York, New Jersey port,

447
00:30:48,560 --> 00:30:51,520
other areas of the harbor call for a more delicate approach.

448
00:30:54,620 --> 00:31:01,620
Specialized equipment must be used to ensure the dredge operations don't release toxins back into the environment.

449
00:31:05,620 --> 00:31:12,620
The Michigan is what's known as a clamshell dredge.

450
00:31:12,621 --> 00:31:17,620
This clamshell bucket has been modified into what's known as an environmental bucket,

451
00:31:17,621 --> 00:31:22,940
bucket, designed specifically for handling light sediment and utilizing special rubber

452
00:31:22,941 --> 00:31:25,020
seals to keep spillage to a minimum.

453
00:31:25,021 --> 00:31:32,020
As the bucket is raised, its speed is also controlled to reduce the amount of overflow.

454
00:31:32,021 --> 00:31:41,540
Candidly, dredging, although it's a technological challenge, is actually the easier part of

455
00:31:41,541 --> 00:31:42,820
a program.

456
00:31:42,821 --> 00:31:48,020
The most important program is where is it that we take the dredge material and what

457
00:31:48,021 --> 00:31:53,080
it is we remediate and will it be acceptable.

458
00:31:53,081 --> 00:31:58,040
One of the ways to render the hazardous material into a form that can be used safely is to

459
00:31:58,041 --> 00:32:03,580
mix the sediment with Portland cement.

460
00:32:03,581 --> 00:32:07,880
Portland cement is a compound that not only hardens when mixed with water, but also becomes

461
00:32:07,881 --> 00:32:08,880
water resistant.

462
00:32:08,881 --> 00:32:11,580
In the treatment process with the cement,

463
00:32:11,680 --> 00:32:15,380
the cement binds the sediment particles together,

464
00:32:15,480 --> 00:32:19,460
and in that process, it traps any contaminated material.

465
00:32:19,560 --> 00:32:21,220
It's typically heavy metals.

466
00:32:21,320 --> 00:32:24,500
It binds that within the cement so it doesn't leach.

467
00:32:25,840 --> 00:32:28,500
The mixture will remain stable over time

468
00:32:28,600 --> 00:32:31,160
and could be used to cover or cap land

469
00:32:31,260 --> 00:32:33,100
that may still contain toxic elements.

470
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:37,540
In this area, we're taking it to a couple of different sites.

471
00:32:37,541 --> 00:32:42,140
We have some park area that is being capped.

472
00:32:42,860 --> 00:32:45,440
They were industrial sites, industrial landfills,

473
00:32:46,020 --> 00:32:48,260
and they're being capped and are going to be made into a park.

474
00:32:49,520 --> 00:32:52,960
The beneficial use of dredged materials also includes

475
00:32:52,961 --> 00:32:55,860
the rebuilding of areas lost to development and erosion.

476
00:32:57,160 --> 00:33:00,780
On the west coast of the U.S., the California State Conservancy

477
00:33:00,781 --> 00:33:05,260
has implemented a 50-year plan in conjunction with the Army Corps of Engineers

478
00:33:05,261 --> 00:33:08,180
to restore wetlands around the San Francisco Bay.

479
00:33:08,840 --> 00:33:11,500
Most of the projects started in the 80s or the 90s,

480
00:33:11,560 --> 00:33:13,940
and they were relatively small, you know, 50 acres here,

481
00:33:14,080 --> 00:33:15,340
maybe a couple hundred acres there.

482
00:33:15,800 --> 00:33:19,880
And now we've gone from that to one of the last major projects we did,

483
00:33:19,980 --> 00:33:22,800
Sonoma Baylands project, was 300 and some odd acres.

484
00:33:23,220 --> 00:33:26,000
And that was with the deepening of the Port of Oakland to 42 feet,

485
00:33:26,260 --> 00:33:27,220
you know, a decade ago.

486
00:33:27,660 --> 00:33:30,300
And now this decade we're deepening the Port of Oakland more,

487
00:33:30,301 --> 00:33:33,260
and we're doing a really truly landscape-scale project.

488
00:33:33,261 --> 00:33:36,220
Hamilton Wetlands Restoration Project is currently 1,000 acres,

489
00:33:36,680 --> 00:33:38,500
and we're trying to expand that to 2,600.

490
00:33:39,840 --> 00:33:43,880
It'll take about 20 years of maintenance dredging in the San Francisco Bay

491
00:33:43,881 --> 00:33:48,860
to amass the 25 million cubic yards needed to restore the Hamilton Wetlands

492
00:33:48,861 --> 00:33:51,840
in San Pablo Bay, north of San Francisco.

493
00:33:53,660 --> 00:33:56,140
You pump it in there, and then it comes out the end of a pipe,

494
00:33:56,560 --> 00:33:59,480
and by managing how you decant the water off the top,

495
00:33:59,780 --> 00:34:02,580
you make the material flow around to get the line and the grade you want.

496
00:34:03,260 --> 00:34:08,260
Dredged material is also being used to restore ocean beaches.

497
00:34:08,261 --> 00:34:13,260
Beachfront construction and breakwaters have drastically altered wave patterns.

498
00:34:13,261 --> 00:34:18,260
This has led in turn to disastrous erosion on many sections of coastline.

499
00:34:18,261 --> 00:34:22,260
In a situation where you don't have the sediments, you just have a seawall,

500
00:34:22,261 --> 00:34:28,260
large waves come in unabated, attack the seawall and cause all that energy turns into turbulence in the near shore zone

501
00:34:28,261 --> 00:34:31,260
and causes greater acceleration of the erosion.

502
00:34:31,261 --> 00:34:37,800
And so seawalls, although they do serve their purpose in the immediate region where they're put in,

503
00:34:38,140 --> 00:34:40,800
eventually will have nothing but an armored coastline.

504
00:34:42,100 --> 00:34:48,780
The Army Corps has placed dredged sand several hundred yards offshore the Ocean Beach area south of San Francisco,

505
00:34:49,500 --> 00:34:54,500
with the hope that natural tidal and wave action will deposit the sand back onto eroded beaches.

506
00:34:54,501 --> 00:35:01,500
After they come into the near shore area, they will bottom dump the sediments into a

507
00:35:01,501 --> 00:35:04,500
berm which is parallel to the shore face.

508
00:35:04,501 --> 00:35:08,500
We hope to get about a thousand foot long berm, maybe about three to four to five feet high,

509
00:35:08,501 --> 00:35:10,500
a couple hundred feet wide.

510
00:35:10,501 --> 00:35:15,500
And then the action of the waves will push that sand into the near shore zone where it

511
00:35:15,501 --> 00:35:17,500
will widen the beach.

512
00:35:17,501 --> 00:35:23,500
And then it'll provide sediments when storms attack the coastline to form offshore sandbars

513
00:35:23,501 --> 00:35:27,700
sandbars and dissipate the wave energy through breaking.

514
00:35:27,701 --> 00:35:30,940
Dredging contributes to land restoration,

515
00:35:30,941 --> 00:35:32,800
but the modern generation of dredges

516
00:35:32,801 --> 00:35:36,980
can also create brand new land.

517
00:35:36,981 --> 00:35:40,480
Mega-sized ports are the way of the future.

518
00:35:40,481 --> 00:35:43,740
In the Los Angeles harbor, the Pier 400 project

519
00:35:43,741 --> 00:35:47,360
created a 600-acre terminal for shipping containers,

520
00:35:47,361 --> 00:35:50,780
the largest such facility in the world.

521
00:35:50,781 --> 00:35:52,580
The job involved a unique partnership

522
00:35:52,581 --> 00:35:55,420
between the Army Corps of Engineers and the Port of Los Angeles.

523
00:35:56,800 --> 00:36:02,180
The Port of L.A.'s interest was to develop a harbor for commerce

524
00:36:02,181 --> 00:36:04,480
and to deepen it to 81 feet.

525
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:08,740
Their objective met our objectives in that we needed a place to put our dredge material,

526
00:36:08,741 --> 00:36:12,820
and they needed to have a landfill, so it worked out perfectly.

527
00:36:14,700 --> 00:36:17,540
Five dredges, led by the workhorse Florida,

528
00:36:18,100 --> 00:36:21,060
were contracted by the Army Corps to deepen the main channel

529
00:36:21,061 --> 00:36:23,900
to between 53 and 81 feet,

530
00:36:24,420 --> 00:36:27,540
deep enough to accommodate any ship in the world today.

531
00:36:30,380 --> 00:36:32,160
The massive scope of the work,

532
00:36:32,280 --> 00:36:34,380
combined with the heavy traffic flow in the harbor,

533
00:36:35,020 --> 00:36:37,200
gave the Army Corps a nearly constant headache.

534
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:42,640
You have about three to five miles of dredge pipe at times

535
00:36:42,641 --> 00:36:44,560
to deal with, and active harbor,

536
00:36:44,561 --> 00:36:46,880
one of the more active harbors in the world.

537
00:36:48,340 --> 00:36:50,520
We could not impede traffic at all.

538
00:36:51,060 --> 00:36:55,140
and maintain safety was a critical aspect of this job.

539
00:36:55,460 --> 00:36:58,620
So having to deal with floating pipe and then submerged pipe,

540
00:36:58,760 --> 00:37:02,420
moving it on a regular basis, was very challenging.

541
00:37:03,580 --> 00:37:05,500
To solve some of those challenges,

542
00:37:05,980 --> 00:37:09,300
the Army Corps turned to its Engineering Research and Development Center

543
00:37:09,301 --> 00:37:10,780
in Vicksburg, Mississippi.

544
00:37:12,240 --> 00:37:16,720
The ERDC occupies 1.7 million square feet

545
00:37:16,721 --> 00:37:19,880
and includes an acre-sized simulation tank

546
00:37:19,881 --> 00:37:24,340
in which a 44,000 square foot model of the Los Angeles Harbor was built.

547
00:37:26,020 --> 00:37:30,620
Experiments tested tidal action and the effect of wave energy on siltation in the harbor.

548
00:37:32,740 --> 00:37:38,020
It took six years of nonstop dredging to create a 500 foot wide channel

549
00:37:38,021 --> 00:37:41,740
that extended three miles out from the harbor into the open ocean.

550
00:37:43,200 --> 00:37:48,720
The Florida and other dredges scooped 60 million cubic yards of material from the ocean bottom

551
00:37:48,721 --> 00:37:50,720
and piped them onto a landfill,

552
00:37:50,721 --> 00:37:53,720
on top of which the new container facility was built.

553
00:37:55,720 --> 00:37:58,720
A small army of dredges is bringing the United States

554
00:37:58,721 --> 00:38:00,720
into the 21st century,

555
00:38:00,721 --> 00:38:03,720
well-equipped to compete in the global market.

556
00:38:04,720 --> 00:38:06,720
But thousands of miles away,

557
00:38:06,721 --> 00:38:10,720
an armada of new dredges, bigger than any ever built,

558
00:38:10,721 --> 00:38:13,720
are transforming the map of our world.

559
00:38:15,720 --> 00:38:17,720
As part of the Pier 400 project,

560
00:38:17,721 --> 00:38:22,000
dredged material was used to fill in 250 acres of the outer harbor

561
00:38:22,001 --> 00:38:26,320
to create a shallow water habitat that supports marine birds and wildlife.

562
00:38:29,720 --> 00:38:33,840
The growing population and superheated economies of the Far East

563
00:38:33,841 --> 00:38:36,140
make land a very precious commodity.

564
00:38:37,600 --> 00:38:41,260
The demand for real estate is fierce, especially near the ocean.

565
00:38:41,261 --> 00:38:48,980
The Hong Kong airport recently opened to air traffic on 2,300 acres of land that didn't

566
00:38:48,981 --> 00:38:52,540
exist 10 years ago.

567
00:38:52,541 --> 00:38:57,760
250 million cubic yards of sand and clay were pulled from the ocean floor to complete the

568
00:38:57,761 --> 00:38:58,760
project.

569
00:38:58,761 --> 00:39:06,680
In Malaysia, hundreds of yards of new property are being added to the shoreline each week.

570
00:39:06,681 --> 00:39:10,060
They had to have the big hopper dredges.

571
00:39:10,061 --> 00:39:18,240
They would come in and go offshore of Indonesia and Malaysia and dredge up the sand, maybe

572
00:39:18,241 --> 00:39:25,820
do a 100 kilometers round trip to get the sand, bring it back to the harbor, and either

573
00:39:25,821 --> 00:39:32,400
spray it ashore or pump it ashore and build the new land.

574
00:39:32,401 --> 00:39:37,100
In the Philippines, state-of-the-art dredging helped build an underwater natural gas facility

575
00:39:37,101 --> 00:39:40,400
35 miles off the coast of Palawan Island.

576
00:39:42,740 --> 00:39:45,380
A foundation of dredged sand and gravel

577
00:39:45,381 --> 00:39:48,000
was poured nearly one-half mile below sea level,

578
00:39:48,420 --> 00:39:51,860
and then a massive concrete structure was positioned on top.

579
00:39:52,840 --> 00:39:54,300
When fully operational,

580
00:39:54,880 --> 00:39:57,320
the plant will be able to fill the energy needs

581
00:39:57,321 --> 00:39:59,380
of the entire nation of the Philippines.

582
00:40:01,020 --> 00:40:03,540
Material used in the creation of new land

583
00:40:03,541 --> 00:40:05,900
must be fine-grained and dense enough

584
00:40:05,901 --> 00:40:08,060
to support the enormous weight of new construction.

585
00:40:10,100 --> 00:40:12,480
As the demand for new land increases,

586
00:40:13,120 --> 00:40:15,960
dredges have to venture further and further out into the ocean

587
00:40:15,961 --> 00:40:18,240
to find construction-quality sand.

588
00:40:20,100 --> 00:40:21,740
To make these trips profitable,

589
00:40:22,480 --> 00:40:24,300
dredges have to get faster and bigger,

590
00:40:25,080 --> 00:40:26,100
much bigger.

591
00:40:27,660 --> 00:40:29,880
When it was launched in the year 2000,

592
00:40:30,380 --> 00:40:33,880
the Vasco da Gama was the largest hopper suction dredge ever built.

593
00:40:33,881 --> 00:40:40,880
It weighs 60,000 tons and is more than 200 yards long.

594
00:40:40,881 --> 00:40:43,880
The drag arms weigh 55 tons each,

595
00:40:43,881 --> 00:40:46,880
and it only takes about an hour to fill the hoppers

596
00:40:46,881 --> 00:40:51,880
with 35,000 cubic yards of ocean bottom.

597
00:40:51,881 --> 00:40:54,880
The Vasco da Gama has eight separate hoppers,

598
00:40:54,881 --> 00:40:58,880
and the operator must carefully control how these are filled.

599
00:40:58,881 --> 00:41:02,880
Loading too much sand too quickly onto one side of the vessel

600
00:41:02,881 --> 00:41:04,680
could cause the ship to capsize.

601
00:41:07,640 --> 00:41:09,840
Once the gargantuan hoppers are full,

602
00:41:10,360 --> 00:41:13,940
two 20,000 horsepower engines can power the Vasco da Gama

603
00:41:13,941 --> 00:41:17,220
back to port at nearly 15 miles per hour.

604
00:41:19,480 --> 00:41:22,800
Its sandy treasure is offloaded in one of three ways.

605
00:41:23,400 --> 00:41:26,020
Through a pipeline up to five miles long,

606
00:41:26,740 --> 00:41:29,620
through the bottom doors, which only takes 15 minutes,

607
00:41:29,621 --> 00:41:34,840
or it's rainbowed, a process that shoots the slurry through the air

608
00:41:34,841 --> 00:41:37,320
into an arc of more than 100 yards.

609
00:41:39,000 --> 00:41:41,780
With the awesome capabilities of the modern dredge,

610
00:41:42,040 --> 00:41:43,860
it was probably only a matter of time

611
00:41:43,861 --> 00:41:46,680
before we came up with some mind-boggling new applications.

612
00:41:48,800 --> 00:41:50,180
In the Persian Gulf,

613
00:41:50,680 --> 00:41:53,520
the world's largest land reclamation project is underway.

614
00:41:55,560 --> 00:41:58,560
In a venture authorized by the royal family of Dubai,

615
00:41:58,561 --> 00:42:05,560
Three islands in the shape of palm fronds are being built a half mile offshore.

616
00:42:05,561 --> 00:42:12,560
12,000 private homes, alongside 100 luxury hotels and restaurants, will adorn these new

617
00:42:12,561 --> 00:42:17,560
islands, which average about 16 square miles each.

618
00:42:17,561 --> 00:42:22,560
In order to do that, they are having to bring in large quantities of sand to build these

619
00:42:22,561 --> 00:42:29,560
these islands, or the palm islands, which are essentially arranged like palm branches on a palm tree.

620
00:42:29,561 --> 00:42:37,560
And so this creates a large area of beach because you've got beaches on both sides of the palm leaves, if you will.

621
00:42:37,561 --> 00:42:47,560
The dredges that are being used to bring in that sand are very large, hauling on the order of 30,000 to 40,000 cubic yards at a time.

622
00:42:47,561 --> 00:42:48,500
It's huge.

623
00:42:48,620 --> 00:42:50,820
It's a 10-year construction project for reclamation

624
00:42:50,821 --> 00:42:53,960
and to build this whole fantasy world that they want over there

625
00:42:53,961 --> 00:42:56,820
to bring in restaurants and tourists and rich people

626
00:42:56,821 --> 00:42:58,500
that want beachfront property condominiums.

627
00:43:00,080 --> 00:43:02,840
And the three islands are just phase one.

628
00:43:03,480 --> 00:43:05,320
They're building islands in certain shapes.

629
00:43:05,940 --> 00:43:10,040
Out in the Gulf there, it looks more like a resort area,

630
00:43:10,160 --> 00:43:13,380
but it has a certain shape that you can recognize from up in the air.

631
00:43:13,381 --> 00:43:22,020
Joining the Palm Islands in Dubai is a group of 300 artificial islets, tiny islands that

632
00:43:22,021 --> 00:43:27,480
when viewed from above will look like a map of the world.

633
00:43:27,481 --> 00:43:32,140
Dozens of these private islands have already been sold at prices ranging upwards of $10

634
00:43:32,141 --> 00:43:34,140
million each.

635
00:43:34,141 --> 00:43:40,640
When complete, this undertaking will have cost over $14 billion and will have created

636
00:43:40,641 --> 00:43:47,640
some 450 miles of brand new coastline in the Persian Gulf.

637
00:43:47,641 --> 00:43:50,400
Such is the awesome power of dredging.

638
00:43:50,401 --> 00:43:55,480
Down and dirty machines that create as easily as they destroy.

639
00:43:55,481 --> 00:44:00,880
Today's dredges are able to do in months what may have taken nature thousands of years.

640
00:44:00,881 --> 00:44:08,140
24 hours of every single day, dredging helps drive the global economy as it transforms

641
00:44:08,141 --> 00:44:08,841
our planet.

